<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Togo</h1><p class="st-meta">1960 CE – present · capital Lomé</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A narrow West African republic, independent from France in 1960, governed by one family since 1967 and by a parliamentary system under its 2024 constitution.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-togoland">French Togoland</a>, a trust territory after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, became an autonomous republic within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-union">French Union</a> in 1956, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> keeping control of defence, foreign relations and finances. The Togolese Republic was proclaimed on 27 April 1960, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylvanus-olympio">Sylvanus Olympio</a> became its first president in 1961.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Olympio banned opposition parties and was assassinated on 13 January 1963 in a military coup, in which Sergeant Gnassingbé Eyadéma took part. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-grunitzky">Nicolas Grunitzky</a> then led the country until Eyadéma overthrew him on 13 January 1967. Eyadéma formed the Rally of the Togolese People, introduced a one-party system in November 1969 and ruled for 38 years. Other parties were allowed in 1991, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> froze its partnership in 1993 after his re-election, and the elections that followed were marked by irregularities.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Eyadéma died on 5 February 2005, and the military installed his son Faure Gnassingbé, whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-union">African Union</a> described as a military coup. Gnassingbé was elected in April 2005 and re-elected in 2010, 2015 and 2020. A new constitution adopted on 6 May 2024 made the president a mostly ceremonial office, and in May 2025 Jean-Lucien Savi de Tové became president while Gnassingbé became president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-european-union">Council of Ministers</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/togo.jpg" alt="Togo" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Sangjinhwa · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Togo_(3-2).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1960 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lomé</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sylvanus Olympio, Nicolas Grunitzky, Gnassingbé Eyadéma, Faure Gnassingbé, Jean-Lucien Savi de Tové</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity, traditional beliefs, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Kabiye, Ewe</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/togo">Togo</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Togolese Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=togo" aria-label="Togo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4714 1948 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Togo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5034 2204L5034 2204"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5034 2204h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5034 2204h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5034 2204h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lomé. <a href="/atlas?year=1993&amp;focus=togo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
